course: dictionary
lesson: 2650
title: Strong feelings in words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words show how strongly someone feels. «Detest» is one of them. It means to hate something very much, far more than just not liking it. Look at the words around it to find the clues.
explain: «The Lord detests dishonest scales, but accurate weights find favour with him.» Dishonest scales cheat people when they weigh shopping, so detest must mean something close to hate. The clue is dishonest: cheating is the kind of thing people strongly hate.
explain: Swap your meaning into the sentence to check. «The Lord hates dishonest scales» still makes sense, so hate is a good match. Ask yourself: is this something you only dislike, or something you really hate?
explain: Detest is a strong word, so most people say «hate» or «can't stand» in everyday talk. You will meet detest more often in stories and formal writing. Detestable describes something so bad that people deserve to detest it.
fact: Detest means to hate something or someone very much.
fact: Detests, detested and detesting are the other forms of the verb.
fact: Detestable is an adjective: it describes something so bad that people deserve to detest it.
fact: «The Lord detests dishonest scales» (Proverbs 11:1) means God strongly hates cheating with weights.
fact: In everyday talk, «hate» and «can't stand» are the usual words; detest is stronger and more formal.
exercise: «The Lord detests dishonest scales.» What does detests mean? | Weighs carefully | *Hates very much | Loves dearly | Sells cheaply
exercise: Ruby says, «I detest cabbage!» What does she feel? | She is pleased with it | *She hates it very much | She has just cooked it | She is very hungry
exercise: Find and copy the word that shows a strong feeling of hate in «The Lord detests them both.» | Lord | them | *detests | both
test: Which word describes something so bad that people deserve to detest it? | *detestable | detests | detested | detesting
test: Which everyday word could replace detest in a sentence? | Wonder | Happy | Sleepy | *Hate
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to detest? | Forgive | Adore | Ignore | *Loathe
